Subject: register VM read forwarding is linear in the instruction count

What this does:
  The forward-a-frame-read pass scanned every instruction once per block to
  find the block's stores, then again to find its reads, and cleared the whole
  slot table at every block -- blocks x instructions and blocks x slots on
  every function. It now buckets the instructions into one contiguous range per
  block (the block index advances monotonically with `src_pc`) and walks each
  range twice, so each instruction is visited a constant number of times, and
  clears only the slots the block actually stored.

  The clobber test is O(1): `lreg` holds the index of the last instruction that
  writes each physical register. It is sized by the register count, not the
  value count, and the buffers use `js_malloc_rt`: the pass is optional, so an
  allocation failure (including the zero-size case) skips it instead of leaving
  a pending out-of-memory exception on the context. A register at or beyond
  `nlreg` is treated as clobbered rather than read out of bounds.

Why we need it:
  The per-block scans made read forwarding quadratic on large functions; encode
  time on pdf.js and mandreel was dominated by it.

Needs a bytecode version bump:
  No. Lowering only.

Notes:
  The first revision of this pass allocated `lreg` with `js_malloc(ctx, 0)` on
  functions with no values, which returns NULL and throws out of memory; the
  module-code test262 failures came from that stray exception, not from the
  forwarding itself.

